{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan review BLOCK on three findings that are all the same excellent point, and it inverted the design.\n\nMy plan said ambient CI was the SOLE condition on the unsandboxed fail-open. That was wrong: wrap() already has a SECOND fail-open branch keyed on REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED (opt_out_enabled). So adding a new config key would have created two overlapping waiver surfaces with divergent semantics — worse than the gap it closed.\n\nThe fix is now SUBTRACTIVE: delete the CI-keyed branch and leave the existing explicit opt-out as the single waiver. Fewer branches, one documented way to disable the sandbox, and ambient CI can no longer do it.\n\nAlso recorded: _mutation.yml runs the gate on a plain ubuntu-latest runner whose kernel denies unprivileged CLONE_NEWUSER, so probe() returns None and the gate runs UNSANDBOXED via the CI branch today — while that step's comment claims 'Without it the gate fails CLOSED'. That lane must declare the waiver explicitly before the branch is removed, or it starts aborting; the comment is corrected too."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787908375580439001,"uuid":"d8feb38a-173e-4b37-b51d-92ec99d0095b"}